#. type: TH
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:7 build/C/man7/glob.7:6
#, no-wrap
msgid "glob"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:10
msgid "glob, globfree - find pathnames matching a pattern, free memory from glob()"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<#include E<lt>glob.hE<gt>>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:21
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<int glob(const char *restrict >I<pattern>B<, int >I<flags>B<,>\n"
"B<         typeof(int (const char *>I<epath>B<, int >I<eerrno>B<)) "
"*>I<errfunc>B<,>\n"
"B<         glob_t *restrict >I<pglob>B<);>\n"
"B<void globfree(glob_t *>I<pglob>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:34
msgid ""
"The B<glob>()  function searches for all the pathnames matching I<pattern> "
"according to the rules used by the shell (see B<glob>(7)).  No tilde "
"expansion or parameter substitution is done; if you want these, use "
"B<wordexp>(3)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:40
msgid ""
"The B<globfree>()  function frees the dynamically allocated storage from an "
"earlier call to B<glob>()."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:51
msgid ""
"The results of a B<glob>()  call are stored in the structure pointed to by "
"I<pglob>.  This structure is of type I<glob_t> (declared in "
"I<E<lt>glob.hE<gt>>)  and includes the following elements defined by POSIX.2 "
"(more may be present as an extension):"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:59
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"typedef struct {\n"
"    size_t   gl_pathc;    /* Count of paths matched so far  */\n"
"    char   **gl_pathv;    /* List of matched pathnames.  */\n"
"    size_t   gl_offs;     /* Slots to reserve in \\f[I]gl_pathv\\fR.  */\n"
"} glob_t;\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:63
msgid "Results are stored in dynamically allocated storage."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:69
msgid ""
"The argument I<flags> is made up of the bitwise OR of zero or more the "
"following symbolic constants, which modify the behavior of B<glob>():"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:69
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_ERR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:77
msgid ""
"Return upon a read error (because a directory does not have read permission, "
"for example).  By default, B<glob>()  attempts carry on despite errors, "
"reading all of the directories that it can."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:77
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_MARK>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:80
msgid "Append a slash to each path which corresponds to a directory."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:80
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_NOSORT>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:85
msgid ""
"Don't sort the returned pathnames.  The only reason to do this is to save "
"processing time.  By default, the returned pathnames are sorted."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:85
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_DOOFFS>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:92
msgid ""
"Reserve I<pglob-E<gt>gl_offs> slots at the beginning of the list of strings "
"in I<pglob-E<gt>pathv>.  The reserved slots contain null pointers."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:92
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_NOCHECK>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:100
msgid ""
"If no pattern matches, return the original pattern.  By default, B<glob>()  "
"returns B<GLOB_NOMATCH> if there are no matches."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:100
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_APPEND>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:107
msgid ""
"Append the results of this call to the vector of results returned by a "
"previous call to B<glob>().  Do not set this flag on the first invocation of "
"B<glob>()."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:107
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_NOESCAPE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:114
msgid ""
"Don't allow backslash (\\[aq]\\[rs]\\[aq]) to be used as an escape "
"character.  Normally, a backslash can be used to quote the following "
"character, providing a mechanism to turn off the special meaning "
"metacharacters."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:118
msgid ""
"I<flags> may also include any of the following, which are GNU extensions and "
"not defined by POSIX.2:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:118
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_PERIOD>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:122
msgid ""
"Allow a leading period to be matched by metacharacters.  By default, "
"metacharacters can't match a leading period."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:122
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_ALTDIRFUNC>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:133
msgid ""
"Use alternative functions I<pglob-E<gt>gl_closedir>, "
"I<pglob-E<gt>gl_readdir>, I<pglob-E<gt>gl_opendir>, I<pglob-E<gt>gl_lstat>, "
"and I<pglob-E<gt>gl_stat> for filesystem access instead of the normal "
"library functions."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:133
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_BRACE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:149
msgid ""
"Expand B<csh>(1)  style brace expressions of the form B<{a,b}>.  Brace "
"expressions can be nested.  Thus, for example, specifying the pattern "
"\"{foo/{,cat,dog},bar}\" would return the same results as four separate "
"B<glob>()  calls using the strings: \"foo/\", \"foo/cat\", \"foo/dog\", and "
"\"bar\"."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:149
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_NOMAGIC>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:154
msgid ""
"If the pattern contains no metacharacters, then it should be returned as the "
"sole matching word, even if there is no file with that name."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:154
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_TILDE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:166
msgid ""
"Carry out tilde expansion.  If a tilde (\\[aq]\\[ti]\\[aq]) is the only "
"character in the pattern, or an initial tilde is followed immediately by a "
"slash (\\[aq]/\\[aq]), then the home directory of the caller is substituted "
"for the tilde.  If an initial tilde is followed by a username (e.g., "
"\"\\[ti]andrea/bin\"), then the tilde and username are substituted by the "
"home directory of that user.  If the username is invalid, or the home "
"directory cannot be determined, then no substitution is performed."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:166
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_TILDE_CHECK>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:177
msgid ""
"This provides behavior similar to that of B<GLOB_TILDE>.  The difference is "
"that if the username is invalid, or the home directory cannot be determined, "
"then instead of using the pattern itself as the name, B<glob>()  returns "
"B<GLOB_NOMATCH> to indicate an error."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:177
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_ONLYDIR>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:190
msgid ""
"This is a I<hint> to B<glob>()  that the caller is interested only in "
"directories that match the pattern.  If the implementation can easily "
"determine file-type information, then nondirectory files are not returned to "
"the caller.  However, the caller must still check that returned files are "
"directories.  (The purpose of this flag is merely to optimize performance "
"when the caller is interested only in directories.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:213
msgid ""
"If I<errfunc> is not NULL, it will be called in case of an error with the "
"arguments I<epath>, a pointer to the path which failed, and I<eerrno>, the "
"value of I<errno> as returned from one of the calls to B<opendir>(3), "
"B<readdir>(3), or B<stat>(2).  If I<errfunc> returns nonzero, or if "
"B<GLOB_ERR> is set, B<glob>()  will terminate after the call to I<errfunc>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:220
msgid ""
"Upon successful return, I<pglob-E<gt>gl_pathc> contains the number of "
"matched pathnames and I<pglob-E<gt>gl_pathv> contains a pointer to the list "
"of pointers to matched pathnames.  The list of pointers is terminated by a "
"null pointer."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:229
msgid ""
"It is possible to call B<glob>()  several times.  In that case, the "
"B<GLOB_APPEND> flag has to be set in I<flags> on the second and later "
"invocations."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:237
msgid ""
"As a GNU extension, I<pglob-E<gt>gl_flags> is set to the flags specified, "
"B<or>ed with B<GLOB_MAGCHAR> if any metacharacters were found."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:242
msgid ""
"On successful completion, B<glob>()  returns zero.  Other possible returns "
"are:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:242
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_NOSPACE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:245
msgid "for running out of memory,"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:245
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_ABORTED>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:248
msgid "for a read error, and"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:248
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<GLOB_NOMATCH>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:251
msgid "for no found matches."
msgstr ""

#. type: tbl table
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:263
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<glob>()"
msgstr ""

#. type: tbl table
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:268
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"MT-Unsafe race:utent env\n"
"sig:ALRM timer locale"
msgstr ""

#. type: tbl table
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:273
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<globfree>()"
msgstr ""

#. type: tbl table
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:273 build/C/man3/regex.3:327 build/C/man3/wordexp.3:197
#, no-wrap
msgid "MT-Safe"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:290
msgid ""
"In the above table, I<utent> in I<race:utent> signifies that if any of the "
"functions B<setutent>(3), B<getutent>(3), or B<endutent>(3)  are used in "
"parallel in different threads of a program, then data races could occur.  "
"B<glob>()  calls those functions, so we use race:utent to remind users."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:294 build/C/man7/glob.7:156
#, no-wrap
msgid "NOTES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:305
msgid ""
"The structure elements I<gl_pathc> and I<gl_offs> are declared as I<size_t> "
"in glibc 2.1, as they should be according to POSIX.2, but are declared as "
"I<int> in glibc 2.0."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:305 build/C/man7/regex.7:341
#, no-wrap
msgid "BUGS"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:314
msgid ""
"The B<glob>()  function may fail due to failure of underlying function "
"calls, such as B<malloc>(3)  or B<opendir>(3).  These will store their error "
"code in I<errno>."
msgstr ""

#. type: SH
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:314 build/C/man3/regex.3:356 build/C/man3/wordexp.3:219
#, no-wrap
msgid "EXAMPLES"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:316
msgid "One example of use is the following code, which simulates typing"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:320
#, no-wrap
msgid "ls -l *.c ../*.c\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:324
msgid "in the shell:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:335
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"glob_t globbuf;\n"
"\\&\n"
"globbuf.gl_offs = 2;\n"
"glob(\"*.c\", GLOB_DOOFFS, NULL, &globbuf);\n"
"glob(\"../*.c\", GLOB_DOOFFS | GLOB_APPEND, NULL, &globbuf);\n"
"globbuf.gl_pathv[0] = \"ls\";\n"
"globbuf.gl_pathv[1] = \"-l\";\n"
"execvp(\"ls\", &globbuf.gl_pathv[0]);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/glob.3:347
msgid ""
"B<ls>(1), B<sh>(1), B<stat>(2), B<exec>(3), B<fnmatch>(3), B<malloc>(3), "
"B<opendir>(3), B<readdir>(3), B<wordexp>(3), B<glob>(7)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:9
msgid "glob - globbing pathnames"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:14
msgid ""
"Long ago, in UNIX\\ V6, there was a program I</etc/glob> that would expand "
"wildcard patterns.  Soon afterward this became a shell built-in."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:18
msgid ""
"These days there is also a library routine B<glob>(3)  that will perform "
"this function for a user program."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:20
msgid "The rules are as follows (POSIX.2, 3.13)."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:20
#, no-wrap
msgid "Wildcard matching"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:27
msgid ""
"A string is a wildcard pattern if it contains one of the characters "
"\\[aq]?\\[aq], \\[aq]*\\[aq], or \\[aq][\\[aq].  Globbing is the operation "
"that expands a wildcard pattern into the list of pathnames matching the "
"pattern.  Matching is defined by:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:29
msgid "A \\[aq]?\\[aq] (not between brackets) matches any single character."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:32
msgid ""
"A \\[aq]*\\[aq] (not between brackets) matches any string, including the "
"empty string."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:34
msgid "B<Character classes>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:47
msgid ""
"An expression \\[dq]I<[...]>\\[dq] where the first character after the "
"leading \\[aq][\\[aq] is not an \\[aq]!\\[aq] matches a single character, "
"namely any of the characters enclosed by the brackets.  The string enclosed "
"by the brackets cannot be empty; therefore \\[aq]]\\[aq] can be allowed "
"between the brackets, provided that it is the first character.  (Thus, "
"\\[dq]I<[][!]>\\[dq] matches the three characters \\[aq][\\[aq], "
"\\[aq]]\\[aq], and \\[aq]!\\[aq].)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:49
msgid "B<Ranges>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:66
msgid ""
"There is one special convention: two characters separated by \\[aq]-\\[aq] "
"denote a range.  (Thus, \\[dq]I<[A-Fa-f0-9]>\\[dq] is equivalent to "
"\\[dq]I<[ABCDEFabcdef0123456789]>\\[dq].)  One may include \\[aq]-\\[aq] in "
"its literal meaning by making it the first or last character between the "
"brackets.  (Thus, \\[dq]I<[]-]>\\[dq] matches just the two characters "
"\\[aq]]\\[aq] and \\[aq]-\\[aq], and \\[dq]I<[--0]>\\[dq] matches the three "
"characters \\[aq]-\\[aq], \\[aq].\\[aq], and \\[aq]0\\[aq], since "
"\\[aq]/\\[aq] cannot be matched.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:68
msgid "B<Complementation>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:78
msgid ""
"An expression \\[dq]I<[!...]>\\[dq] matches a single character, namely any "
"character that is not matched by the expression obtained by removing the "
"first \\[aq]!\\[aq] from it.  (Thus, \\[dq]I<[!]a-]>\\[dq] matches any "
"single character except \\[aq]]\\[aq], \\[aq]a\\[aq], and \\[aq]-\\[aq].)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:89
msgid ""
"One can remove the special meaning of \\[aq]?\\[aq], \\[aq]*\\[aq], and "
"\\[aq][\\[aq] by preceding them by a backslash, or, in case this is part of "
"a shell command line, enclosing them in quotes.  Between brackets these "
"characters stand for themselves.  Thus, \\[dq]I<[[?*\\[rs]]>\\[dq] matches "
"the four characters \\[aq][\\[aq], \\[aq]?\\[aq], \\[aq]*\\[aq], and "
"\\[aq]\\[rs]\\[aq]."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:89
#, no-wrap
msgid "Pathnames"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:97
msgid ""
"Globbing is applied on each of the components of a pathname separately.  A "
"\\[aq]/\\[aq] in a pathname cannot be matched by a \\[aq]?\\[aq] or "
"\\[aq]*\\[aq] wildcard, or by a range like \\[dq]I<[.-0]>\\[dq].  A range "
"containing an explicit \\[aq]/\\[aq] character is syntactically incorrect.  "
"(POSIX requires that syntactically incorrect patterns are left unchanged.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:109
msgid ""
"If a filename starts with a \\[aq].\\[aq], this character must be matched "
"explicitly.  (Thus, I<rm\\ *> will not remove I<.profile>, and I<tar\\ c\\ "
"*> will not archive all your files; I<tar\\ c\\ .> is better.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:109
#, no-wrap
msgid "Empty lists"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:115
msgid ""
"The nice and simple rule given above: \"expand a wildcard pattern into the "
"list of matching pathnames\" was the original UNIX definition.  It allowed "
"one to have patterns that expand into an empty list, as in"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:118
#, no-wrap
msgid "    xv -wait 0 *.gif *.jpg\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:128
msgid ""
"where perhaps no *.gif files are present (and this is not an error).  "
"However, POSIX requires that a wildcard pattern is left unchanged when it is "
"syntactically incorrect, or the list of matching pathnames is empty.  With "
"I<bash> one can force the classical behavior using this command:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:132
#, no-wrap
msgid "shopt -s nullglob\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:138
msgid "(Similar problems occur elsewhere.  For example, where old scripts have"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:142
#, no-wrap
msgid "rm \\`find . -name \"*\\[ti]\"\\`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:146
msgid "new scripts require"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:150
#, no-wrap
msgid "rm -f nosuchfile \\`find . -name \"*\\[ti]\"\\`\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:156
msgid "to avoid error messages from I<rm> called with an empty argument list.)"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:157
#, no-wrap
msgid "Regular expressions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:164
msgid ""
"Note that wildcard patterns are not regular expressions, although they are a "
"bit similar.  First of all, they match filenames, rather than text, and "
"secondly, the conventions are not the same: for example, in a regular "
"expression \\[aq]*\\[aq] means zero or more copies of the preceding thing."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:170
msgid ""
"Now that regular expressions have bracket expressions where the negation is "
"indicated by a \\[aq]\\[ha]\\[aq], POSIX has declared the effect of a "
"wildcard pattern \\[dq]I<[\\[ha]...]>\\[dq] to be undefined."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:170
#, no-wrap
msgid "Character classes and internationalization"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:194
msgid ""
"Of course ranges were originally meant to be ASCII ranges, so that "
"\\[dq]I<[\\ -%]>\\[dq] stands for \\[dq]I<[\\ !\\[dq]#$%]>\\[dq] and "
"\\[dq]I<[a-z]>\\[dq] stands for \"any lowercase letter\".  Some UNIX "
"implementations generalized this so that a range X-Y stands for the set of "
"characters with code between the codes for X and for Y.  However, this "
"requires the user to know the character coding in use on the local system, "
"and moreover, is not convenient if the collating sequence for the local "
"alphabet differs from the ordering of the character codes.  Therefore, POSIX "
"extended the bracket notation greatly, both for wildcard patterns and for "
"regular expressions.  In the above we saw three types of items that can "
"occur in a bracket expression: namely (i) the negation, (ii) explicit single "
"characters, and (iii) ranges.  POSIX specifies ranges in an internationally "
"more useful way and adds three more types:"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:200
msgid ""
"(iii) Ranges X-Y comprise all characters that fall between X and Y "
"(inclusive) in the current collating sequence as defined by the "
"B<LC_COLLATE> category in the current locale."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:202
msgid "(iv) Named character classes, like"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:207
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[:alnum:]  [:alpha:]  [:blank:]  [:cntrl:]\n"
"[:digit:]  [:graph:]  [:lower:]  [:print:]\n"
"[:punct:]  [:space:]  [:upper:]  [:xdigit:]\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:220
msgid ""
"so that one can say \\[dq]I<[[:lower:]]>\\[dq] instead of "
"\\[dq]I<[a-z]>\\[dq], and have things work in Denmark, too, where there are "
"three letters past \\[aq]z\\[aq] in the alphabet.  These character classes "
"are defined by the B<LC_CTYPE> category in the current locale."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:232
msgid ""
"(v) Collating symbols, like \\[dq]I<[.ch.]>\\[dq] or "
"\\[dq]I<[.a-acute.]>\\[dq], where the string between \\[dq]I<[.>\\[dq] and "
"\\[dq]I<.]>\\[dq] is a collating element defined for the current locale.  "
"Note that this may be a multicharacter element."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:247
msgid ""
"(vi) Equivalence class expressions, like \\[dq]I<[=a=]>\\[dq], where the "
"string between \\[dq]I<[=>\\[dq] and \\[dq]I<=]>\\[dq] is any collating "
"element from its equivalence class, as defined for the current locale.  For "
"example, \\[dq]I<[[=a=]]>\\[dq] might be equivalent to "
"\\[dq]I<[a\\('a\\(`a\\(:a\\(^a]>\\[dq], that is, to "
"\\[dq]I<[a[.a-acute.][.a-grave.][.a-umlaut.][.a-circumflex.]]>\\[dq]."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man7/glob.7:252
msgid "B<sh>(1), B<fnmatch>(3), B<glob>(3), B<locale>(7), B<regex>(7)"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:7
#, no-wrap
msgid "re_comp"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:10
msgid "re_comp, re_exec - BSD regex functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:18
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<#define _REGEX_RE_COMP>\n"
"B<#include E<lt>sys/types.hE<gt>>\n"
"B<#include E<lt>regex.hE<gt>>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:21
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<[[deprecated]] char *re_comp(const char *>I<regex>B<);>\n"
"B<[[deprecated]] int re_exec(const char *>I<string>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:34
msgid ""
"B<re_comp>()  is used to compile the null-terminated regular expression "
"pointed to by I<regex>.  The compiled pattern occupies a static area, the "
"pattern buffer, which is overwritten by subsequent use of B<re_comp>().  If "
"I<regex> is NULL, no operation is performed and the pattern buffer's "
"contents are not altered."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:40
msgid ""
"B<re_exec>()  is used to assess whether the null-terminated string pointed "
"to by I<string> matches the previously compiled I<regex>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:45
msgid ""
"B<re_comp>()  returns NULL on successful compilation of I<regex> otherwise "
"it returns a pointer to an appropriate error message."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:48
msgid "B<re_exec>()  returns 1 for a successful match, zero for failure."
msgstr ""

#. type: tbl table
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:61
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<re_comp>(),\n"
"B<re_exec>()"
msgstr ""

#. type: tbl table
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:61
#, no-wrap
msgid "MT-Unsafe"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:65
msgid "None."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:67
msgid "4.3BSD."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:72
msgid ""
"These functions are obsolete; the functions documented in B<regcomp>(3)  "
"should be used instead."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/re_comp.3:75
msgid "B<regcomp>(3), B<regex>(7), GNU regex manual"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:7 build/C/man7/regex.7:29
#, no-wrap
msgid "regex"
msgstr ""

#. type: TH
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:7
#, no-wrap
msgid "2026-02-22"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:10
msgid "regcomp, regexec, regerror, regfree - POSIX regex functions"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:16
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<#include E<lt>regex.hE<gt>>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:23
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<int regcomp(regex_t *restrict >I<preg>B<, const char *restrict "
">I<regex>B<,>\n"
"B<            int >I<cflags>B<);>\n"
"B<int regexec(const regex_t *restrict >I<preg>B<,>\n"
"B<            const char *restrict >I<string>B<,>\n"
"B<            size_t >I<n>B<, regmatch_t >I<pmatch>B<[_Nullable restrict "
">I<n>B<],>\n"
"B<            int >I<eflags>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:29
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<size_t regerror(>size_t errbuf_size;\n"
"B<            int >I<errcode>B<, const regex_t *_Nullable restrict "
">I<preg>B<,>\n"
"B<            char >I<errbuf>B<[_Nullable restrict >I<errbuf_size>B<],>\n"
"B<            size_t >I<errbuf_size>B<);>\n"
"B<void regfree(regex_t *>I<preg>B<);>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:33
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<typedef struct {>\n"
"B<size_t    re_nsub;>\n"
"B<} regex_t;>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:38
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"B<typedef struct {>\n"
"B<regoff_t  rm_so;>\n"
"B<regoff_t  rm_eo;>\n"
"B<} regmatch_t;>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:40
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<typedef> /* ... */  B<regoff_t;>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:42
#, no-wrap
msgid "Compilation"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:48
msgid ""
"B<regcomp>()  is used to compile a regular expression into a form that is "
"suitable for subsequent B<regexec>()  searches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:56
msgid ""
"On success, the pattern buffer at I<*preg> is initialized.  I<regex> is a "
"null-terminated string.  The locale must be the same when running "
"B<regexec>()."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:71
msgid ""
"After B<regcomp>()  succeeds, I<preg-E<gt>re_nsub> holds the number of "
"subexpressions in I<regex>.  Thus, a value of I<preg-E<gt>re_nsub> + 1 "
"passed as I<n> to B<regexec>()  is sufficient to capture all matches."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:76
msgid "I<cflags> is the bitwise OR of zero or more of the following:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:76
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<REG_EXTENDED>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:85
msgid ""
"Use POSIX Extended Regular Expression syntax when interpreting I<regex>.  If "
"not set, POSIX Basic Regular Expression syntax is used."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:85
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<REG_ICASE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:91
msgid ""
"Do not differentiate case.  Subsequent B<regexec>()  searches using this "
"pattern buffer will be case insensitive."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:91
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<REG_NOSUB>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:101
msgid ""
"Report only overall success.  B<regexec>()  will use only I<pmatch> for "
"B<REG_STARTEND>, ignoring I<n>."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:101
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<REG_NEWLINE>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:104
msgid "Match-any-character operators don't match a newline."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:108
msgid ""
"A nonmatching list (B<[\\[ha]...\\&]>)  not containing a newline does not "
"match a newline."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:118
msgid ""
"Match-beginning-of-line operator (B<\\[ha]>)  matches the empty string "
"immediately after a newline, regardless of whether I<eflags>, the execution "
"flags of B<regexec>(), contains B<REG_NOTBOL>."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:126
msgid ""
"Match-end-of-line operator (B<$>)  matches the empty string immediately "
"before a newline, regardless of whether I<eflags> contains B<REG_NOTEOL>."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:126
#, no-wrap
msgid "Matching"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:137
msgid ""
"B<regexec>()  is used to match a null-terminated string against the compiled "
"pattern buffer in I<*preg>, which must have been initialised with "
"B<regcomp>().  I<eflags> is the bitwise OR of zero or more of the following "
"flags:"
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:137
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<REG_NOTBOL>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:147
msgid ""
"The match-beginning-of-line operator always fails to match (but see the "
"compilation flag B<REG_NEWLINE> above).  This flag may be used when "
"different portions of a string are passed to B<regexec>()  and the beginning "
"of the string should not be interpreted as the beginning of the line."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:147
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<REG_NOTEOL>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:153
msgid ""
"The match-end-of-line operator always fails to match (but see the "
"compilation flag B<REG_NEWLINE> above)."
msgstr ""

#. type: TP
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:153
#, no-wrap
msgid "B<REG_STARTEND>"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:173
msgid ""
"Match [I<string + pmatch[0].rm_so>,I< string + pmatch[0].rm_eo>)  instead of "
"[I<string>,\\~I<strnul(string)>).  This allows matching embedded NUL bytes.  "
"If any matches are returned (B<REG_NOSUB> wasn't passed to B<regcomp>(), the "
"match succeeded, and I<n> E<gt> 0), they overwrite I<pmatch> as usual, and "
"the match offsets remain relative to I<string> (not I<string + "
"pmatch[0].rm_so>).  This flag is a BSD extension, not present in POSIX."
msgstr ""

#. type: SS
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:173
#, no-wrap
msgid "Match offsets"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:199
msgid ""
"Unless B<REG_NOSUB> was passed to B<regcomp>(), it is possible to obtain the "
"locations of matches within I<string>: B<regexec>()  fills I<n> elements of "
"I<pmatch> with results: I<pmatch[0]> corresponds to the entire match, "
"I<pmatch[1]> to the first subexpression, etc.  If there were more matches "
"than I<n>, they are discarded; if fewer, unused elements of I<pmatch> are "
"filled with B<-1>s."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:204
msgid ""
"Each returned valid (non-B<-1>)  match corresponds to the range [I<string + "
"rm_so>,I< string + rm_eo>)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: build/C/man3/regex.3:212
msgid ""
"I<regoff_t> is a signed integer type capable of storing the largest value "
"that can be stored in either an I<ptrdiff_t> type or a I<ssize_t> type."
msgstr ""

